The LHCb experiment conducted a model-dependent amplitude analysis of the $B^0\rightarrow D(K^0_S\pi^+\pi^-) K^{*0}$ decay using 3.0 fb$^{-1}$ of proton-proton collision data collected at $\sqrt{s}=7$ and $8$ TeV. This study measured the CP violation observables $x_{\pm}$ and $y_{\pm}$, which are sensitive to the CKM angle $\gamma$, yielding the following results:
\begin{eqnarray*} x_- &=& -0.15 \pm 0.14 \pm 0.03 \pm 0.01, \quad y_- &=& 0.25 \pm 0.15 \pm 0.06 \pm 0.01, \\ x_+ &=& 0.05 \pm 0.24 \pm 0.04 \pm 0.01, \quad y_+ &=& -0.65^{+0.24}_{-0.23} \pm 0.08 \pm 0.01, \end{eqnarray*}
where the uncertainties listed are, respectively, statistical, systematic, and due to the $D\rightarrow K^0_S \pi^+\pi^-$ amplitude model. These represent the most precise measurements of these observables to date. The results correspond to $\gamma=(80^{+21}_{-22})^{\circ}$ and $r_{B^0}=0.39\pm0.13$, with $r_{B^0}$ denoting the magnitude of the ratio between the suppressed and favoured $B^0\rightarrow D K^+ \pi^-$ decay amplitudes. These findings are specific to the $K\pi$ mass region within $\pm50$ MeV of the $K^*(892)^0$ mass and for $K^{*0}$ decay angles where the absolute value of the cosine exceeds 0.4.

Let $\psi$ be a Parseval wavelet in $L^2(\mathbb{R})$ generating the subspace of negative dilates $V(\psi)$. The intersection of these dilates constitutes the trivial subspace. Specifically, this is expressed as:
\begin{align*}
  \bigcap_{n\in\Z} D^n \overline{\textrm{span}}\{D^{\textrm{-}m} T^\ell \psi
\mid m\geq 0, m,\ell\in\Z\}=\{0\}.
\end{align*}

We examine the stochastic differential equation (SDE) given by:
\begin{equation}
  X_t=X_0+\int_0^tb(X_s,\theta_0)ds+\sigma B_t,~~~t\in(0,T], \end{equation}
in which $\{B_t\}_{t\ge 0}$ denotes a fractional Brownian motion characterized by a Hurst index $H\in(1/2,1)$. The parameter $\theta_0$ is associated with a bounded, open, and convex subset $\Theta\subset\mathbb{R}^d$. The term $\{b(\cdot,\theta),\theta\in\Theta\}$ represents a collection of drift coefficients defined as functions $b(\cdot,\theta):\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$, while $\sigma\in\mathbb{R}$ stands for the known diffusion coefficient.

We examine a periodic nonlinear Schrödinger equation featuring white noise dispersion and a power-type nonlinearity defined by the equation:
\begin{equation*}
idu = \Delta u \circ dW_t + |u|^{p-1}u\;dt
\end{equation*}
Through the establishment of stochastic Strichartz estimates, we demonstrate almost sure global well-posedness for this equation with $L^2$ initial data when the nonlinearity exponent satisfies $1 < p \leq 3$. Furthermore, by extending Fourier restriction spaces $X^{s,b}$ to a stochastic framework, we verify that our solutions coincide with those previously constructed by Chouk and Gubinelli via rough path methods. Additionally, we analyze the quintic case ($p=5$), proving that it is analytically ill-posed within the space $L^1_\omega C_t L^2_x$.
